From 06055e82f42f744342b62f49656cad280730742a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 12:32:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] feat: landscape-aware scoring

Photos without prominent faces were judged by portrait rules: mean-brightness
exposure punished sunsets and snow, global Laplacian variance made crisp scenes
with empty sky look blurry, and subject/composition were a flat neutral 50.

- SceneAnalyzer (OpenCV/NumPy, thread-safe): horizon line (Hough, mode-clustered,
  signed tilt + position + confidence), highlight/shadow clipping, dynamic range,
  upper-quartile tiled sharpness, spectral-residual saliency centroid
- classify_scene: people / landscape / general. A face only makes a photo
  'people' when large enough to be the subject (scene.people_min_face_area)
- Landscapes use clipping-based exposure, dynamic range instead of contrast,
  tiled sharpness (never lower than global), and horizon level/placement +
  focal-point composition. Subject is excluded from the weighted mean
- Soft-focus gate caps landscapes at 'review' so good composition cannot
  rescue an out-of-focus frame (thresholds.soft_focus_sharpness)
- Report schema 3 (additive; schema 2 still loads): scene_type, scene_counts,
  CSV 'scene' column, terminal scene mix line

Non-landscape scoring is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
