From d8584d8361190e400ecdd196d6bd52f8573df0e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 18:55:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] feat: group-photo subject detection and blink-aware
 best-shot selection

A group photo was scored by averaging every detected face, so a passer-by in the
background could drag it down, while a subject with fully closed eyes still
scored ~80 (the weighted sum barely penalised it). Best-shot choice used the final
score alone, including the AI label, which flips between near-identical frames.

- scoring/group.py: a face is a subject if it is at least selection.min_face_area
  of the frame AND selection.relative_face_area of the largest face, so passers-by
  are ignored but a distant group is still a group. Eyes are only judged on faces
  big enough to read and not in profile (better to miss a blink than invent one)
- Closed eyes cap that face (45) and the whole subject score (60); subject and
  composition are computed on subjects only, so a cropped bystander no longer
  hurts composition
- Scores.selection: final score without the AI component. Frames of one scene
  share content, so selection compares them on technical/subject/composition
- select_best_shots: within selection.margin points of the best frame, the one
  with fewest problems wins (closed eyes, or a subject missing vs the group's
  median face count so one false detection cannot skew it); outside the margin
  quality wins, so nobody keeps a blurry frame just for open eyes. Reasons explain
  why each frame was kept or dropped
- thresholds.closed_eyes_gate caps photos with a closed-eyed subject at 'review'
- Report: CSV subjects/closed_eyes columns, summary.closed_eyes_photos

Changed existing test: test_two_faces_weight_the_weaker_one used an eye ratio of
0.10, which now counts as closed and is capped; it now uses 0.16 (a squint) to keep
testing the 40/60 weighting.

