From 04c6ab3e45d791e63db955885fad57e5e979167b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 01:32:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] feat: reliable burst culling from EXIF time, appearance
 and sharpness

Grouping used perceptual-hash distance alone, so frames of a burst that moved
or panned more than 8 bits were never grouped, and best-shot choice could be
decided by the AI label (which flips between near-identical frames) rather than
by motion blur.

- Capture time from EXIF DateTimeOriginal/Digitized with sub-seconds (bursts fall
  inside one second). File mtime is deliberately not used: copying resets it and
  a wrong time is worse than none
- Burst links: a frame joins any frame within bursts.max_gap_seconds that looks
  continuous (hamming <= bursts.hamming_threshold, looser than duplicates). Looking
  back over a window, not just the previous frame, keeps a burst intact when
  another photographer's frames interleave. Chains are cut at max_span_seconds
- Groups are union-find over appearance clusters and burst links, with stable ids.
  Each gets a kind (duplicate = byte-identical, burst, similar) and time span.
  Frames without capture time still group by appearance exactly as before
- Selection compares frames on relative sharpness: a frame much softer than the
  group's sharpest loses up to selection.sharpness_weight points. Absolute sharpness
  saturates when every frame is soft or crisp, so it cannot tell a smeared frame
  from a good one. Landscapes use the tiled measure. Reasons now say when a frame
  was dropped for being soft
- Report: captured_at/group_kind CSV columns, summary burst_groups/burst_frames,
  TL;DR mentions bursts

